WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.980
To ensure the accuracy and

2
00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.215
reliability of the
data in your database,

3
00:00:04.215 --> 00:00:05.910
you must limit the
type of data they

4
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.935
can go into your database table.

5
00:00:07.935 --> 00:00:09.990
In this video,
you'll learn how to

6
00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.060
describe the purpose
of constraints in

7
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.800
a database and identify

8
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.130
default constraints to set
default values in a table.

9
00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.380
Database constraints
are used to limit

10
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.780
the type of data that can
be stored in a table.

11
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.060
This ensures that
all data inserted

12
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.610
into the table is
accurate and reliable.

13
00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.980
If the database
dictates a violation

14
00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.380
between the constraint
and the data operations,

15
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.825
then it aborts these operations.

16
00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.360
An example of a violation
might be an attempt to

17
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.225
insert or upload invalid
data to a table.

18
00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:40.950
The database realizes that

19
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.075
the data is invalid
and rejects it.

20
00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:45.200
Constraints can be
column level where

21
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.405
the rule applies to
a specific column.

22
00:00:47.405 --> 00:00:49.670
They can also be
applied at table level.

23
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.320
For example, I could use

24
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.315
the foreign key
constraints to prevent

25
00:00:53.315 --> 00:00:55.855
actions that will destroy
links between tables.

26
00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.730
I'll demonstrate this in more
detail in a later lesson.

27
00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.130
Two of the most used
database constraints

28
00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:02.720
include NOT NULL,

29
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.330
a method of preventing
empty value fields,

30
00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:09.050
and DEFAULT, a method of
assigning default values.

31
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.720
For now, let's begin
our exploration of

32
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.585
default values with a
NOT NULL constraint.

33
00:01:14.585 --> 00:01:17.600
The NOT NULL SQL constraint
is used to ensure

34
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.040
the data fields are always

35
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.295
completed and never left blank.

36
00:01:21.295 --> 00:01:22.955
Let's explore this concept

37
00:01:22.955 --> 00:01:24.680
using the example
of a table from

38
00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.450
an online store that records

39
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:28.670
the IDs and names of customers.

40
00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.310
The table records this data in

41
00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:34.645
the customer ID and
customer name columns.

42
00:01:34.645 --> 00:01:37.515
These columns must
always contain data.

43
00:01:37.515 --> 00:01:38.880
If there's no data or

44
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.330
values inserted into
either of these columns,

45
00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:44.795
then the creation of a new
customer record is aborted.

46
00:01:44.795 --> 00:01:47.210
The NOT NULL default value is

47
00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.600
implemented using
a SQL statement.

48
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.520
A typical NOT NULL
SQL statement begins

49
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.595
with the creation of a basic
table in the database.

50
00:01:55.595 --> 00:01:58.310
I can write a create
table clause,

51
00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.935
followed by customer to
define the table name,

52
00:02:00.935 --> 00:02:03.190
followed by a pair
of parenthesis.

53
00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:06.265
Within the parentheses
I add two columns;

54
00:02:06.265 --> 00:02:08.915
customer ID and customer name.

55
00:02:08.915 --> 00:02:12.215
I also define each column
with relevant data types.

56
00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:15.520
Int for customer ID as it
stores numeric values,

57
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.415
and varchar for customer name
as it stores string values.

58
00:02:19.415 --> 00:02:21.140
Finally, I also declare

59
00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.580
a NOT NULL constraint
for each column.

60
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.580
This makes sure
that neither column

61
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.485
will accept no values.

62
00:02:27.485 --> 00:02:29.750
Now, any operation
that attempts to place

63
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.000
a null value in these
columns will fail,

64
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.340
like inserting or updating data.

65
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:35.810
Next, let's look at how

66
00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.125
the DEFAULT constraint
works in a table.

67
00:02:38.125 --> 00:02:39.920
The DEFAULT constraints sets

68
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.420
a default value for a column
of no value is specified.

69
00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.860
This means that if no
data is entered for

70
00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.930
a specific field
within a column,

71
00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.429
then the table
will automatically

72
00:02:49.429 --> 00:02:51.595
insert a default value instead.

73
00:02:51.595 --> 00:02:54.410
To gain a better understanding
of default values,

74
00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.150
let's look at a table that holds

75
00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:59.245
player records for a
football club's database.

76
00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.010
The table is called player

77
00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.275
table and contains two columns.

78
00:03:03.275 --> 00:03:05.480
The first is player name and

79
00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.775
lists the names of each
player in the team,

80
00:03:07.775 --> 00:03:09.680
and the second
column is city and

81
00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.995
list which city each
player is from.

82
00:03:11.995 --> 00:03:14.790
Most of the players in this
club are from Barcelona,

83
00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.790
so I can specify a default value

84
00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:18.890
of the city column as Barcelona.

85
00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.260
This means that I don't
have to enter Barcelona

86
00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.340
repeatedly into the city
field for each new player.

87
00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.765
If no value is
entered in the table,

88
00:03:26.765 --> 00:03:28.790
then each field is
automatically filled

89
00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.220
with a default
value of Barcelona.

90
00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:33.560
Let's find out how the
default command is

91
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.885
incorporated into
a SQL statement.

92
00:03:35.885 --> 00:03:38.540
First, I use the
create table commands

93
00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:41.320
to create a table and
then call it player.

94
00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.780
Then within a pair
of parenthesis

95
00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.785
I input the column names,

96
00:03:45.785 --> 00:03:48.080
assign a string
datatype for each,

97
00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.910
and assign a default value of

98
00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:52.225
NOT NULL for the name column.

99
00:03:52.225 --> 00:03:55.480
Finally, I add the
default keyword statement

100
00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.140
followed by the default value

101
00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:59.545
Barcelona for the city column.

102
00:03:59.545 --> 00:04:02.870
Now when I add data into
the table for a new player,

103
00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.520
I don't need to
type in Barcelona

104
00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:06.500
for players who
are from the city.

105
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.430
Instead, it will be
inserted automatically.

106
00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.090
You should then be familiar with

107
00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:13.700
the importance of using
database constraints.

108
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:15.470
You should also be
able to explain

109
00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.300
database constraints
as a method of

110
00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:21.840
enforcing rules on a column
or table level. Go to work.